 ],
 "literal": "Listen! May you live, friend! In our town, / those who unite those who separated, were they there? / Those with staffs in hand, those with white-headed headcloths, / with the people who say \"Good! Good!\" / today, \"It is great!\" saying, the assemblies of that place indeed.",
 "translation": "Listen, my friend, may you live long! In our town, were there ever people who could reunite those who have separated? Today, in the assemblies of that place, the elders holding staffs in their hands and wearing white headcloths are saying, 'Good, very good!' and 'This is great!'",
 "modern": "தோழியே, கேள்! நீ வாழ்க! நம் ஊரில் பிரிந்தவர்களைச் சேர்த்து வைப்பவர்கள் யாராவது இருந்தார்களா? கையில் தடியை ஏந்திய, நரைத்த தலையில் தலைப்பாகை அணிந்த பெரியவர்கள், 'மிகவும் நன்று, நன்று' என்று பாராட்டி, 'இன்று இது மிகப்பெரிய காரியம்' என்று பேசும் அந்த ஊர்ச் சபைகள் இன்று உள்ளனவே!",
 "explanation": "The friend (thozhi) is speaking to the heroine (thalaivi) when the hero's marriage proposal is being discussed and accepted by the town elders. She wonders aloud if there were ever people in their town who could bring separated lovers back together, celebrating that it has finally happened. She points out that today, the wise elders of the town assembly, who carry staffs and wear white headcloths, are praising the union, saying \"Good, good!\" and \"This is a great thing!\" The poem belongs to the Kuriñci tiṇai (mountainous landscape), which deals with the union of lovers. The key words are 'பிரிந்தோர்ப் புணர்ப்போர்' (those who unite the separated) and 'ஆங்கண தவையே' (the assemblies of that place), highlighting the transition from secret love to public, celebrated marriage.",
